---
import BlogArticle from "../../components/BlogArticle.astro";
---

<BlogArticle
	title="How to Convert a Signature to JPG for a PAN Card"
	description="Convert a scanned PAN card signature to a clear JPG/JPEG, preserve the original handwriting, and check the current application’s size and upload requirements."
	canonicalUrl="https://nsdlphotoresize.com/blogs/convert-pan-signature-to-jpg/"
	heading="How to convert a signature to JPG for a PAN card"
	intro="A signature file should be a clear image of the applicant’s real signature, saved in the format and dimensions the application currently accepts. Changing a filename extension is not the same as converting an image."
>
	<section class="space-y-3">
		<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">Make a clean source image</h2>
		<p>
			Sign naturally on plain white paper using the ink and background
			specified by the application. Scan the paper or photograph it
			straight-on in even light. Keep the complete signature in frame,
			with a small margin around the strokes. If using a phone photo,
			avoid shadows, glare, and perspective distortion.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="space-y-3">
		<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">Convert to JPG/JPEG</h2>
		<ol class="list-decimal space-y-3 pl-6">
			<li>Open the <a class="text-link" href="/#photo-tool">PAN card signature resizer</a> in your browser and choose the scan or photo.</li>
			<li>Select Signature mode and choose a portal preset only if it matches the instructions for your current application.</li>
			<li>Crop to the signature and its surrounding margin. Do not cut off pen strokes or stretch the image.</li>
			<li>Export the result as JPEG, then save the downloaded file to your device.</li>
			<li>Check file details to confirm it is a JPEG and is within the current dimensions and file-size limit. Open it to make sure the signature remains legible.</li>
		</ol>
		<p>
			JPG and JPEG refer to the same image format. PNG or WebP can be
			converted by exporting to JPEG, but simply renaming
			<code>.png</code> to <code>.jpg</code> does not convert the
			underlying data and may cause an upload error.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="space-y-3">
		<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">Keep the real signature intact</h2>
		<p>
			Compression is for file size, not for changing handwriting. Do
			not redraw, trace, add strokes, remove parts of the signature, or
			alter it to resemble another person’s signature. Avoid excessive
			contrast or sharpening that erases thin ink marks. If it looks
			faint, make a better scan rather than artificially reconstructing
			it.
		</p>
	</section>

	<p>
		Older guides may quote a 50 KB Protean/NSDL signature or a 60 KB
		UTIITSL signature. Treat those as legacy limits, not guaranteed
		current rules. Read the instructions on the live Form 93/94
		application. This in-browser PAN card document resizer online
		processes your file on your device and does not submit it to a PAN
		portal.
	</p>
</BlogArticle>
